device-note = A device chosen here is played through whatever the desktop routes other sound to; following the system default moves with the desktop's own choice.
renderers-note = Looking asks the local network every minute for UPnP and DLNA renderers (a streamer, a networked amplifier) and browses it for Google Cast receivers (a Chromecast, a smart speaker), and lists each one beside the devices above. Off, nothing is sent to the network.
flac-renderers-note = A renderer switched on here is sent FLAC at 176.4 and 192 kHz as well, which one that states no rates is otherwise not. Switch it on only if it decodes them: a renderer that cannot play a stream goes silent rather than saying so. Read when the renderer is next found, so from the next start.
learnt-formats-note = Learn what it plays tries the device with a few seconds of silence in each format, so it may play nothing for up to a minute, and offers it what it played at once. A device playing a stream is not tried.
no-devices-to-learn = No UPnP renderer or Google Cast receiver is listed. Look for renderers on the network above, and each one found can be tried here.
learn-what-it-plays = Learn what it plays
trying-it = Trying it
forget-what-was-learnt = Forget what was learnt
not-tried-yet = Not tried yet
played-none-of-them = Played none of the formats tried
plays = Plays { $formats }
flac-at-192 = FLAC at 192 kHz
flac-at-176 = FLAC at 176.4 kHz
flac-at-384 = FLAC at 384 kHz
flac-at-352 = FLAC at 352.8 kHz
six-channels = 5.1 at 48 kHz
eight-channels = 7.1 at 48 kHz
six-channels-at-96 = 5.1 at 96 kHz
eight-channels-at-96 = 7.1 at 96 kHz
played = played
untried = not tried
refused = refused
never-fetched = never fetched
stopped-fetching = stopped fetching
errored = failed
not-playing = never played
clock-still = its clock stood still
heard = { $trial }: { $verdict }
unlearnt-not-listed = It is no longer on the network.
unlearnt-in-use = It is playing a stream, so it was not tried; stop it and try again.
unlearnt-unreached = It could not be reached, so nothing was learnt.
no-renderers-to-name = No UPnP renderer is listed. Look for renderers on the network above, and each one found can be named here.
not-on-the-network = Not on the network now
away = The device named in the settings file is not in the graph, so the system default is playing until it appears.
dop-warning = A DAC that does not decode DoP plays it as full-scale white noise. Turn it on only for hardware you know takes it.
unadvertised = Advertises no format of its own
unplugged = Unplugged
default = Default
in-use = In use
converts = Converts
follow = Follow the system default
on-the-hardware = Volume on the hardware
in-software = Volume in software
match-the-file = Match the file
follow-the-graph = Follow the graph
the-stream-opens-at-the-files = The stream opens at the file's own rate wherever the device takes it, so a 44.1 kHz album reaches a DAC untouched rather than resampled to 48 kHz.
ask-the-graph-to-switch = Ask the graph to switch
leave-it-to-the-daemon = Leave it to the daemon
the-stream-asks-pipewire-to-run = The stream asks PipeWire to run the whole graph at its rate while it plays, so nothing between here and the device resamples it. Other sounds playing at the same time are resampled to match instead.
pipewire-keeps-the-rate-it-chose = PipeWire keeps the rate it chose, and anything it cannot take as it stands is converted, here or by the daemon.
n-100-ms = 100 ms
n-250-ms = 250 ms
n-500-ms = 500 ms
a-volume-or-equaliser-change-is = A volume or equaliser change is heard almost at once, but a busy machine has the least room before the sound breaks up.
quick-to-answer-with-room-for = Quick to answer, with room for a moment's load on the machine.
room-for-a-busy-machine-or = Room for a busy machine or a slow disc without a break in the sound; a volume change is still heard within half a second.
the-most-room-for-a-busy = The most room for a busy machine or a network share, at the cost of up to a second before a volume or equaliser change is heard.
n-750-ms = 750 ms
n-1-min = 1 min
n-5-min = 5 min
n-15-min = 15 min
n-1-hour = 1 hour
keep-bluetooth-headphones-from-cutting-the = Keep Bluetooth headphones from cutting the start
off-a-paused-bluetooth-device-is = Off, a paused Bluetooth device is let go and wakes on the music
lead-in = Lead-in
kept-awake-through-a-pause = Kept awake through a pause
look-for-renderers-on-the-network = Look for renderers on the network
off-only-this-computers-own-devices = Off, only this computer's own devices are listed
turn-the-devices-own-volume = Turn the device's own volume
scale-the-samples-when-this-is = Scale the samples when this is off
the-device-playing-now-has-a = The device playing now has a volume of its own.
the-device-playing-now-has-no = The device playing now has no volume of its own, so the stream still turns it.
hand-dsd-to-the-device-as = Hand DSD to the device as DoP
decimate-it-to-pcm-when-this = Decimate it to PCM when this is off
decimate-dsd-to-the-level-of = Decimate DSD to the level of PCM
off-a-dsd-master-plays-some = Off, a DSD master plays some 6 dB quieter than the same music in PCM
no-sinks = No sinks are present in { $system }.
bluetooth-note = Experimental, and only for a device { $system } names as Bluetooth. It spends the headphones' battery to keep their radio up through a pause.
routed = Whatever { $system } routes to at the time
routed-now = Whatever { $system } routes to, which is { $sink } now
following-the-graph-meaning = The stream opens at the rate { $system } is already running at, and a file at any other rate is resampled here, with the filter chosen under Processing.
known-as = The graph knows it as { $name }
takes = Takes { $formats }
kilohertz = { $rates } kHz
milliseconds = { $milliseconds } ms
lead-in-meaning = A stream that starts after the link has slept opens on { $lead_in } of silence before the track. Raise it if the first words are still cut, lower it if the wait is felt.
minutes = { $minutes } min
seconds = { $seconds } s
kept-awake-meaning = A pause keeps the headphones fed with silence for { $kept }, so playing again is heard at once; after that they are let go to sleep and save their battery.
held-by-another = Held bit-perfect by { $zone }
takes-as-is = Takes { $quality } as is
said-and-held-by = { $said }, held bit-perfect by { $zone }
